Since [Python 3.3](https://docs.python.org/3/whatsnew/3.3.html#abc) a bug was fixed meaning the `property()` decorator is now correctly identified as abstract when applied to an abstract method.

Note: Order matters, you have to use `@property` above `@abstractmethod`

**Python 3.3+:** ([python docs](https://docs.python.org/3/library/abc.html#abc.abstractproperty)):

```
from abc import ABC, abstractmethod

class C(ABC):
    @property
    @abstractmethod
    def my_abstract_property(self):
        ...
```

**Python 2:** ([python docs](https://docs.python.org/2/library/abc.html#abc.abstractproperty))

```
from abc import ABCMeta, abstractproperty

class C:
    __metaclass__ = ABCMeta

    @abstractproperty
    def my_abstract_property(self):
        ...
```

